    "known_traps": [
      "Every QBO ledger date in this file is 01/DD/YYYY (MM/DD/YYYY format). Since month is always '01', a parser that assumes DD/MM/YYYY (the Indian default) instead of detecting the file's actual locale will silently reinterpret every date as a different month (e.g. 01/08/2026 -> Aug 1 instead of Jan 8). This cascades into spurious 'timing difference' tags or outright non-matches across the whole ledger, not just one row.",
      "HDFC file has 5 letterhead/metadata rows before the real header row, and the account number+IFSC+branch are crammed into a single comma-separated line (row 4) -- a naive 'skip N rows' approach will break the moment statement length varies.",
      "Several legitimate transactions (filler rows) share narration keywords like 'NEFT TO' with no ledger counterpart by design (GST payments, rent, SaaS subscriptions, ATM withdrawals) -- these are real operating expenses, not reconciliation failures, and should not show up as 'exceptions needing review' once correctly classified as non-AR/AP.",
      "X4a/X4b are a deliberate collision case: identical amount, identical date, identical counterparty, but genuinely two different invoices and two different bank reference numbers. A matching engine keyed only on (amount, date, counterparty) without using the reference number will either double-link one bank row to both invoices or randomly drop one.",
      "X5 is a NEFT credit that gets reversed the next day. If your ingestion treats every credit row as revenue without netting against same-counterparty-near-date debit reversals, this will be wrongly auto-matched as a paid invoice when no such invoice exists."
    ]
  },
